tudy the  underlying research is often prompted by  the researchers themselves and only  later do companies join as partners  but right now we think that vaccines are  our number one tool to exit the  pandemics  finland has one of europe's highest  vaccination rates with over 77 percent  of the population now fully inoculated  this includes adolescents over the age  of 12. there's a national recommendation  that they get the jab  like many other countries finland has  started to gradually lift remaining  covert 19 restrictions in the hope that  the nordic nation could soon return to  something resembling pre-pandemic life  if all goes well the finnish nasal spray  study will move to clinical trials this  autumn  but it will take months if not years to  determine whether the spray works  efficiently for humans  meanwhile mecula and her team will  continue their work  they're trying to get their nasal spray  labeled as a medical device rather than  a new drug to fast track its approval  our science reporter derek williams has  been following what scientists and  researchers have been doing during the  pandemic and as always he's here to  answer your questions  [Music]  this time elise h wants to know what's  happening with vaccines that can be  given via nasal sprays  the more than 20 covid19 vaccines that  are currently approved for use in in  some part of the world induce immunity  in a variety of different ways but they  all have one thing in common which is  that they're injected into a recipient's  muscle tissue  with a needle the body's immune system  therefore learns to recognize sars cov2  not where it would ordinarily encounter  at first  in the eyes or the nose or the throat  but in the upper arm  the shots provide fundamental systemic  protection but they don't prevent future  infection entirely  breakthrough infections in vaccinated  people have grown common since the onset  of the omicron variant  some experts think that to make the  immune system better at preventing  infection  and transmission  we need to develop vaccines that train  what are called your mucosal cells to  recognize the virus  mucosal cells are the cells that line  the upper reaches  of your respiratory tract so  it's the first place that your insides  come into contact with any viruses that  you might inhale  and the best way to train those cells so  the theory would be with a vaccine  that's sprayed or inhaled  rather than one that's injected  in the past researchers have tried to  design intranasal vaccines for other  respiratory diseases like influenza but  they're challenging for a couple of  reasons so success has been  pretty limited um one problem is that to  get the desired result in mucosal cells  nasal vaccine developers often choose to  use what are called  live attenuated viruses and  it can be really pretty tricky to get  the levels of those  just right  and because your nose is a lot closer to  your brain  than your upper arm is if there's an  inflammation or or other problems caused  by the vaccine there  then it's kind of by definition a more  dangerous situation  that said i i do think that intranasal  vaccines will eventually play a role in  controlling  the current pandemic but  what i think will have an even bigger  impact long term is the groundbreaking  research into this type of technology  that was driven by covet 19 which  could theoretically be deployed to fight  other respiratory diseases in the future  thanks to the pandemic working from home  is now part of everyday life in many  parts of the world for large and small  companies this meant upgrading computer  systems new software many young people  saw a huge opportunity and learned to  code to write programs for the new world  of work  in nigeria many responded quickly and  this entrepreneurship opened  professional opportunities including  with some international companies  work on his own terms  before the pandemic dairy had a regular  9-5 job as a software engineer in a  nigerian company  that required him going to an office  every day  but now he works remotely for a company  in europe  making what he made in one year in a  single month  the pandemics like our dollar store  my friends like it's like a blessing in  disguise for me to be honest because  like i said earlier like i never thought  um i could walk remotely i like i never  like i never envisaged that to be honest  until when the pandemic happened two  times a week dary meets with a small  group of fellow software engineers  called polytope labs  they come together to learn the latest  software technology  they all have remote jobs but they also  want to be part of what they believe is  the future of tech  sharon langley is the founder  he says software is africa's new oil  you know we have a lot of people here we  have a lot of bright minds here um  it's the only the only like opportunity  for us is to train these minds and make  them relevant  on like the global stage  members of polytope are part of a small  pool of nigerian senior software  developers that are in high demand all  over the world  so due to the pandemic and because most  companies were forced to start working  remotely most companies now come to  understand that you really do not need  your people to be in the office so it  has really  expanded the opportunities that are  available to talents down here um and it  also opened our eyes to see  what are those necessary skills we need  to start having we need to start  building ourselves on such that we can  now tap into these global opportunities  and everybody is happy  rita achebe is a talent recruiting  manager at talent ql  talent ql was created at the start of  the pandemic to match african tech  talents with international companies  rita says that they receive requests  from about a hundred companies every  month for senior developer roles  but many times it's difficult to fill  these roles because most software  developers in africa don't have the  relevant experience so they are not able  to take advantage of the vast  opportunities available  in order to close this gap talent ql  recently launched a digital academy  called old school  first of we start by deciding what  does the market what are the skills out  there that people are hiring for because  it doesn't make sense to  educate people on skills that are not  needed out there that is what a lot of  some of our traditional institutions do  all the lessons for old school are  recorded in this studio and then  uploaded to the online learning platform  around 8 000 people from 20 countries  applied for the program  6 000 got in  the goal is to train 1 million africans  in 5 years  if the african tech scene continues with  its current momentum  tech talents could become the  continent's biggest exports  the coronavirus pandemic has presented  us with huge challenges and we've  responded with innovations and  initiatives big and small maybe you also  have an idea up your sleeve then bring  it on even the smallest things can help  overcome big crises  next week we look at the economic  fallout of the pandemic  so until then goodbye from us and see  you soon  you
 

